        {
          "id": "deada589-3099-36b8-9f33-3aaf780d0e20",
          "content": {
            "id": "deada589-3099-36b8-9f33-3aaf780d0e20",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "Mercedes reports plunge in Q3 operating profit, hit by job cuts",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: Mercedes-Benz reported stronger than expected margins at its core autos business Wednesday (October 29).</p><p>The German auto giant saw improved sales of premium models.</p><p>That helped offset falling sales in China and one-off charges related to ongoing job cuts.</p><p>Mercedes faces particular challenges in the Chinese premium and luxury market, like its rivals Porsche and BMW.</p><p>Local carmakers have driven a price war which has hit demand.</p><p>CEO Ola Kaellenius said the carmaker didn't want to join the price war despite the difficulties it faces in China.</p><p>U.S. import tariffs have also weighed on auto companies.</p><p>Mercedes-Benz's return on sales at its car division was 4.8% in the third quarter, up from last year and well ahead of analyst expectations.</p><p>This was helped by a 10% increase in top-end models, while free cash flow was about $1.6 billion.</p><p>It led the automaker to resume its share buyback program.</p><p>However, operating profit fell 70% owing to charges related to layoffs.</p><p>The company is implementing restructuring measures to save around $5.8 billion globally by 2027.</p><p>Shares in the company were up 6% in early trade to hit a seven-month high.</p><p>Mercedes' challenges are spread across its most important markets.</p><p>It faces tariffs in the U.S., and a highly competitive Chinese market.</p><p>While European emissions targets have led to a challenging shift towards electric vehicles.</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: Mercedes-Benz reported stronger than expected margins at its core autos business Wednesday (October 29).</p><p>The German auto giant saw improved sales of premium models.</p><p>That helped offset falling sales in China and one-off charges related to ongoing job cuts.</p><p>Mercedes faces particular challenges in the Chinese premium and luxury market, like its rivals Porsche and BMW.</p><p>Local carmakers have driven a price war which has hit demand.</p><p>CEO Ola Kaellenius said the carmaker didn't want to join the price war despite the difficulties it faces in China.</p><p>U.S. import tariffs have also weighed on auto companies.</p><p>Mercedes-Benz's return on sales at its car division was 4.8% in the third quarter, up from last year and well ahead of analyst expectations.</p><p>This was helped by a 10% increase in top-end models, while free cash flow was about $1.6 billion.</p><p>It led the automaker to resume its share buyback program.</p><p>However, operating profit fell 70% owing to charges related to layoffs.</p><p>The company is implementing restructuring measures to save around $5.8 billion globally by 2027.</p><p>Shares in the company were up 6% in early trade to hit a seven-month high.</p><p>Mercedes' challenges are spread across its most important markets.</p><p>It faces tariffs in the U.S., and a highly competitive Chinese market.</p><p>While European emissions targets have led to a challenging shift towards electric vehicles.</p></body>",
